1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:01,519
A camp, at night.

2
00:00:01,519 --> 00:00:05,299
Two drones circle above it, each with a thermal camera.

3
00:00:06,183 --> 00:00:11,858
An intruder is found, tracked, and both drones fire so that their rounds arrive together.

4
00:00:12,750 --> 00:00:16,291
You can also climb into the tank yourself, and fire.

5
00:00:17,750 --> 00:00:23,839
This is ThermalSense, a final project of the Computer Graphics Laboratory at KUET.

6
00:00:23,839 --> 00:00:27,181
Almost everything in it is generated by code.

7
00:00:27,181 --> 00:00:31,637
The camp, the vehicles and the soldiers have no model files.

8
00:00:31,637 --> 00:00:34,013
There is no mathematics library.

9
00:00:34,013 --> 00:00:37,206
No shader calls a built-in vector function.

10
00:00:37,206 --> 00:00:39,211
Even the sound is computed.

11
00:00:40,117 --> 00:00:42,632
This film goes from zero to the end.

12
00:00:42,632 --> 00:00:51,018
For every step you will first see the idea, drawn one stage at a time, and then what it does inside the running program.

13
00:00:51,917 --> 00:00:53,864
The program draws one place:

14
00:00:53,864 --> 00:00:57,134
a military outpost about seventy metres across.

15
00:00:57,134 --> 00:01:00,820
A command bunker sits under the ground in the middle.

16
00:01:00,820 --> 00:01:11,671
Around it are a helipad, a radar, an anti-aircraft gun, missile and rocket launchers, three tanks, a barracks hall, guard towers, and a fence with one gate.

17
00:01:12,583 --> 00:01:13,861
Here it is running.

18
00:01:13,861 --> 00:01:16,552
This is not a recording of a film scene:

19
00:01:16,552 --> 00:01:19,579
the camera is free, and you can fly anywhere.

20
00:01:19,579 --> 00:01:26,642
Soldiers live and work here, two drones are parked on their pads, and every lamp, door and vehicle works.

21
00:01:27,550 --> 00:01:28,911
Come down to the road.

22
00:01:28,911 --> 00:01:33,737
Through the gate, past the flag, towards the ramp that leads under the ground.

23
00:01:34,650 --> 00:01:36,012
Escape opens a menu.

24
00:01:36,012 --> 00:01:42,686
From it you can travel to any unit, set the time of day, change the view, and adjust the settings.

25
00:01:42,686 --> 00:01:46,703
More than a hundred and twenty keys are listed in the help.

26
00:01:47,617 --> 00:01:53,159
The film follows one frame through the program, in the order the program builds it.

27
00:01:53,159 --> 00:01:54,160
Geometry first.

28
00:01:54,160 --> 00:01:55,228
Then the camera.

29
00:01:55,228 --> 00:01:55,963
Then light.

30
00:01:55,963 --> 00:02:03,775
Then the world that moves, the controls, the thermal camera, and the three kinds of agents that think for themselves:

31
00:02:03,775 --> 00:02:06,646
the drones, the intruders and the soldiers.

32
00:02:07,550 --> 00:02:08,453
Question one.

33
00:02:08,453 --> 00:02:11,232
A graphics card can only draw triangles.

34
00:02:11,232 --> 00:02:15,955
How do you get a tank out of triangles, without a modelling program?

35
00:02:16,850 --> 00:02:18,587
Start with one triangle:

36
00:02:18,587 --> 00:02:22,133
three corners, each with a position and a normal.

37
00:02:22,133 --> 00:02:24,521
Two triangles make a square face.

38
00:02:24,521 --> 00:02:26,113
Six faces make a cube.

39
00:02:26,113 --> 00:02:36,967
A face shares its corners between its two triangles, so the cube stores twenty-four corners and thirty-six indices instead of thirty-six full corners.

40
00:02:37,883 --> 00:02:41,236
A curved shape is the same idea with more pieces.

41
00:02:41,236 --> 00:02:43,905
A sphere is cut into slices and stacks.

42
00:02:43,905 --> 00:02:46,436
With eight slices it is a rough ball.

43
00:02:46,436 --> 00:02:57,248
With thirty-six slices and eighteen stacks it has six hundred and fourteen corners and one thousand two hundred and twenty-four triangles, and it looks round.

44
00:02:57,248 --> 00:02:59,985
The counts come from two short formulas.

45
00:03:00,883 --> 00:03:05,286
The program has sixteen such generators, and nothing else.

46
00:03:05,286 --> 00:03:06,121
Box shapes:

47
00:03:06,121 --> 00:03:09,308
cube, cuboid, wedge, tapered box, pyramid.

48
00:03:09,308 --> 00:03:10,295
Round shapes:

49
00:03:10,295 --> 00:03:12,420
sphere, hemisphere, capsule.

50
00:03:12,420 --> 00:03:14,546
Shapes turned about an axis:

51
00:03:14,546 --> 00:03:16,367
cylinder, frustum, cone.

52
00:03:16,367 --> 00:03:16,823
Rings:

53
00:03:16,823 --> 00:03:18,493
torus, annulus, helix.

54
00:03:18,493 --> 00:03:20,086
And two special ones:

55
00:03:20,086 --> 00:03:22,515
a blade of grass and the ground.

56
00:03:23,417 --> 00:03:28,829
In the program, the F key recolours every part by the generator that made it.

57
00:03:28,829 --> 00:03:29,813
Blue is a box.

58
00:03:29,813 --> 00:03:31,219
Green is a cylinder.

59
00:03:31,219 --> 00:03:32,554
Orange is a sphere.

60
00:03:32,554 --> 00:03:39,865
The whole camp is five thousand four hundred and seventy-four parts, and each one is one of the sixteen.

61
00:03:40,750 --> 00:03:45,571
The G key pulls every object apart into its parts, and puts it back.

62
00:03:47,750 --> 00:03:50,535
And X shows the triangles themselves:

63
00:03:50,535 --> 00:03:54,299
first with hidden lines removed, then as an x-ray.

64
00:03:55,750 --> 00:03:57,653
So an object is only a list.

65
00:03:57,653 --> 00:03:58,332
The drone:

66
00:03:58,332 --> 00:04:00,642
one hundred and fifty-eight parts.

67
00:04:00,642 --> 00:04:08,593
The body first, then the arms, the motors and rotors, then the camera, the searchlight and the rounds on their rails.

68
00:04:09,483 --> 00:04:13,793
The tank is two hundred and fifty-five parts, built the same way:

69
00:04:13,793 --> 00:04:16,246
hull, tracks and wheels, turret, gun.

70
00:04:17,150 --> 00:04:18,401
What was compared:

71
00:04:18,401 --> 00:04:21,945
loading finished models, or generating every shape.

72
00:04:21,945 --> 00:04:22,431
Chosen:

73
00:04:22,431 --> 00:04:24,794
generate everything that is built.

74
00:04:24,794 --> 00:04:36,051
Sixteen generators, sixty-two designs, five thousand four hundred and seventy-four parts, and every coordinate is a number in the source code that can be checked.

75
00:04:36,950 --> 00:04:41,215
Every generator makes its shape one unit big, at the origin.

76
00:04:41,215 --> 00:04:44,556
A part becomes a real thing through one matrix:

77
00:04:44,556 --> 00:04:46,973
scale it, rotate it, then move it.

78
00:04:46,973 --> 00:04:48,253
The order matters.

79
00:04:48,253 --> 00:04:52,447
Scale, rotate, translate puts a drone arm where it belongs.

80
00:04:52,447 --> 00:04:57,707
Translate first and then rotate, and the arm swings away round the origin.

81
00:04:58,617 --> 00:05:00,353
Parts hang on each other.

82
00:05:00,353 --> 00:05:03,409
A rotor blade is placed relative to its hub.

83
00:05:03,409 --> 00:05:08,132
The hub is placed relative to the arm, the arm relative to the body.

84
00:05:08,132 --> 00:05:13,271
The world matrix of the blade is the product of all of them, parent first.

85
00:05:13,271 --> 00:05:17,369
Turn the hub, and the blade follows without any extra code.

86
00:05:18,283 --> 00:05:21,426
The H key draws the local axes of the nodes.

87
00:05:21,426 --> 00:05:25,141
On a drone you can see the frames inside each other:

88
00:05:25,141 --> 00:05:29,355
the body, and inside it the two frames of the camera mount.

89
00:05:30,250 --> 00:05:32,501
The radar turns on its own frame.

90
00:05:32,501 --> 00:05:35,434
The dish tilts inside the frame that turns.

91
00:05:36,750 --> 00:05:38,879
A soldier is the deepest one:

92
00:05:38,879 --> 00:05:41,302
seventy-five parts, seven levels.

93
00:05:41,302 --> 00:05:42,991
Hip, thigh, shin, foot.

94
00:05:42,991 --> 00:05:46,222
When he walks, only the joint angles change.

95
00:05:47,117 --> 00:05:48,472
What was compared:

96
00:05:48,472 --> 00:05:51,784
moving every part by itself, or a hierarchy.

97
00:05:51,784 --> 00:05:52,311
Chosen:

98
00:05:52,311 --> 00:05:57,506
a hierarchy, so that one angle on a parent moves everything below it.

99
00:05:58,417 --> 00:05:59,354
Question two.

100
00:05:59,354 --> 00:06:03,033
The camp now exists as numbers in three dimensions.

101
00:06:03,033 --> 00:06:04,259
A screen is flat.

102
00:06:04,259 --> 00:06:07,505
How does a point in the world become a pixel?

103
00:06:08,417 --> 00:06:09,659
First, the camera.

104
00:06:09,659 --> 00:06:11,592
It is given by three things:

105
00:06:11,592 --> 00:06:15,458
where the eye is, what it looks at, and which way is up.

106
00:06:15,458 --> 00:06:26,849
From these the program builds three axes, right, up and forward, and one matrix that moves the whole world so that the eye sits at the origin, looking down one axis.

107
00:06:27,750 --> 00:06:28,924
Then projection.

108
00:06:28,924 --> 00:06:32,595
A point twice as far away should look half as big.

109
00:06:32,595 --> 00:06:35,457
So x and y are divided by the distance.

110
00:06:35,457 --> 00:06:41,697
The pyramid that the camera sees is squeezed into a cube, from minus one to plus one.

111
00:06:41,697 --> 00:06:46,762
This table follows one real corner of a drone arm through every step:

112
00:06:46,762 --> 00:06:50,065
world, view, clip, the divide, and the pixel.

113
00:06:50,950 --> 00:06:56,309
In the program, Control P changes the projection while everything keeps running.

114
00:06:56,309 --> 00:06:57,649
This is perspective.

115
00:06:58,550 --> 00:07:00,499
Orthographic, from the top:

116
00:07:00,499 --> 00:07:00,932
a map.

117
00:07:00,932 --> 00:07:04,468
No division by distance, so nothing gets smaller.

118
00:07:04,468 --> 00:07:05,550
From the front.

119
00:07:05,550 --> 00:07:06,561
From the side.

120
00:07:06,561 --> 00:07:11,179
And isometric, where the three axes are drawn at the same scale.

121
00:07:13,550 --> 00:07:14,172
Compared:

122
00:07:14,172 --> 00:07:17,145
perspective and three parallel projections.

123
00:07:17,145 --> 00:07:17,629
Chosen:

124
00:07:17,629 --> 00:07:22,260
perspective for playing, because it is how an eye and a camera see.

125
00:07:22,260 --> 00:07:26,201
The parallel views stay in the program as teaching views.

126
00:07:27,117 --> 00:07:30,018
Not everything is in front of the camera.

127
00:07:30,018 --> 00:07:32,849
A line that leaves the view must be cut.

128
00:07:32,849 --> 00:07:36,458
Cohen and Sutherland give each end a four-bit code:

129
00:07:36,458 --> 00:07:38,297
above, below, left, right.

130
00:07:38,297 --> 00:07:39,430
Both codes zero:

131
00:07:39,430 --> 00:07:40,420
keep the line.

132
00:07:40,420 --> 00:07:41,340
A shared bit:

133
00:07:41,340 --> 00:07:42,331
throw it away.

134
00:07:42,331 --> 00:07:47,426
Otherwise, Liang and Barsky find the part of the line that stays inside.

135
00:07:48,317 --> 00:07:52,818
And when two surfaces cover the same pixel, which one do you see?

136
00:07:52,818 --> 00:07:59,051
The depth buffer keeps, for every pixel, the distance of the nearest surface drawn so far.

137
00:07:59,051 --> 00:08:02,098
A new fragment is kept only if it is nearer.

138
00:08:02,983 --> 00:08:05,984
With the Z key the depth test is switched off.

139
00:08:05,984 --> 00:08:10,745
Now whatever is drawn last wins, and the bunker shows through the ground.

140
00:08:10,745 --> 00:08:13,223
Switched on again, the scene is solid.

141
00:08:14,117 --> 00:08:17,472
This is the depth buffer itself, drawn as grey:

142
00:08:17,472 --> 00:08:19,471
white is near, black is far.

143
00:08:20,617 --> 00:08:21,265
Compared:

144
00:08:21,265 --> 00:08:24,936
sorting the parts back to front, or a depth buffer.

145
00:08:24,936 --> 00:08:25,440
Chosen:

146
00:08:25,440 --> 00:08:33,502
a twenty-four bit depth buffer, with the near plane at five centimetres and the far plane at six hundred metres.

147
00:08:34,417 --> 00:08:37,765
A triangle now has three corners on the screen.

148
00:08:37,765 --> 00:08:39,689
Which pixels does it cover?

149
00:08:39,689 --> 00:08:44,178
For each edge there is a function that is positive on one side.

150
00:08:44,178 --> 00:08:47,456
A pixel is inside when all three are positive.

151
00:08:47,456 --> 00:08:52,443
The same three numbers also say how to mix the colours of the corners.

152
00:08:53,350 --> 00:08:56,457
The program can show this on its own frame.

153
00:08:56,457 --> 00:09:02,528
The back-quote key freezes one frame and replays how it was made, in sixteen stages.

154
00:09:02,528 --> 00:09:04,913
Here the corners are transformed.

155
00:09:05,817 --> 00:09:09,657
Here the triangles are filled, scan line by scan line.

156
00:09:13,317 --> 00:09:13,754
Depth.

157
00:09:13,754 --> 00:09:14,263
Shadow.

158
00:09:14,263 --> 00:09:14,555
Fog.

159
00:09:14,555 --> 00:09:17,541
Then the tone curve, and the final frame.

160
00:09:26,317 --> 00:09:30,753
It also has a pixel inspector and a small image laboratory:

161
00:09:30,753 --> 00:09:33,911
blur and edge kernels, a histogram, gamma.

162
00:09:34,817 --> 00:09:35,517
Compared:

163
00:09:35,517 --> 00:09:41,271
no anti-aliasing, more samples per pixel, and reusing the previous frames.

164
00:09:41,271 --> 00:09:41,815
Chosen:

165
00:09:41,815 --> 00:09:46,870
both together, four samples per pixel and temporal anti-aliasing.

166
00:09:47,783 --> 00:09:48,843
Question three.

167
00:09:48,843 --> 00:09:52,021
Every pixel now knows which surface it shows.

168
00:09:52,021 --> 00:09:53,292
What colour is it?

169
00:09:53,292 --> 00:09:55,058
That is decided by light.

170
00:09:55,950 --> 00:09:57,764
Take one point on a surface.

171
00:09:57,764 --> 00:09:58,801
It has a normal.

172
00:09:58,801 --> 00:10:02,819
There is a direction to the light, and a direction to the eye.

173
00:10:02,819 --> 00:10:04,633
Ambient light is a constant.

174
00:10:04,633 --> 00:10:09,039
Diffuse light depends on the angle between the normal and the light.

175
00:10:09,039 --> 00:10:14,093
The highlight depends on the angle between the normal and the half-way vector.

176
00:10:14,093 --> 00:10:15,001
Add the three.

177
00:10:15,917 --> 00:10:19,789
None of this uses the shading language's own functions.

178
00:10:19,789 --> 00:10:23,521
Dot product, normalise, reflect, refract, mix, clamp:

179
00:10:23,521 --> 00:10:28,801
each one is written out in the project and placed in front of every shader.

180
00:10:28,801 --> 00:10:33,026
The compile step refuses a shader that calls a built-in one.

181
00:10:33,917 --> 00:10:38,016
Control L shows one term at a time, in the running scene.

182
00:10:38,016 --> 00:10:38,951
Ambient only.

183
00:10:39,917 --> 00:10:40,812
Diffuse only:

184
00:10:40,812 --> 00:10:42,051
the shape appears.

185
00:10:42,051 --> 00:10:43,015
Specular only:

186
00:10:43,015 --> 00:10:44,392
just the highlights.

187
00:10:44,392 --> 00:10:45,975
And all three together.

188
00:10:49,917 --> 00:10:54,511
The camp has four kinds of light, and keys one to four switch them.

189
00:10:54,511 --> 00:10:55,129
The moon.

190
00:10:55,129 --> 00:10:56,980
The red beacon on the mast.

191
00:10:56,980 --> 00:10:58,146
The searchlights.

192
00:10:58,146 --> 00:11:03,015
And forty-three small lamps, each one a point light with its own range.

193
00:11:04,917 --> 00:11:06,889
Where is the light computed?

194
00:11:06,889 --> 00:11:08,157
Once per triangle:

195
00:11:08,157 --> 00:11:10,834
flat shading, and you see every facet.

196
00:11:10,834 --> 00:11:13,370
At the corners, then blended across:

197
00:11:13,370 --> 00:11:16,893
Gouraud shading, smooth but with a dull highlight.

198
00:11:16,893 --> 00:11:19,781
Or at every pixel, with a blended normal:

199
00:11:19,781 --> 00:11:20,767
Phong shading.

200
00:11:21,683 --> 00:11:24,730
On the helicopter, the O key cycles them.

201
00:11:24,730 --> 00:11:25,102
Flat.

202
00:11:25,102 --> 00:11:25,696
Gouraud.

203
00:11:25,696 --> 00:11:26,142
Phong.

204
00:11:31,683 --> 00:11:38,169
And B switches the highlight model itself, from the classic Blinn-Phong to a microfacet model.

205
00:11:39,683 --> 00:11:40,333
Compared:

206
00:11:40,333 --> 00:11:44,516
flat, Gouraud and Phong shading, and two highlight models.

207
00:11:44,516 --> 00:11:45,021
Chosen:

208
00:11:45,021 --> 00:11:47,906
Phong shading with the microfacet model.

209
00:11:47,906 --> 00:11:52,739
Flat shading is kept only for parts that are meant to look faceted.

210
00:11:53,650 --> 00:11:55,362
Surfaces are not smooth.

211
00:11:55,362 --> 00:12:01,641
Instead of more triangles, the normal is bent by a small pattern computed in the shader.

212
00:12:01,641 --> 00:12:04,423
The same wall, without and with relief.

213
00:12:05,317 --> 00:12:06,852
A shadow is a question:

214
00:12:06,852 --> 00:12:08,787
can this point see the light?

215
00:12:08,787 --> 00:12:12,792
The scene is first drawn from the light, keeping only depth.

216
00:12:12,792 --> 00:12:17,797
Then each pixel compares its own distance to the light with the stored one.

217
00:12:17,797 --> 00:12:24,538
The edge is softened by averaging nearby tests, wider where the shadow falls further from its caster.

218
00:12:25,450 --> 00:12:26,857
Shadows off, and on.

219
00:12:26,857 --> 00:12:30,165
Then the soft darkening in corners, off and on.

220
00:12:35,450 --> 00:12:38,008
Light in the scene has a huge range:

221
00:12:38,008 --> 00:12:41,632
the sun is thousands of times brighter than a lamp.

222
00:12:41,632 --> 00:12:53,711
The program keeps these real values, adapts the exposure like an eye, and only at the very end bends them through a tone curve and gamma into the range a screen can show.

223
00:12:54,617 --> 00:12:55,700
Fog off, and on.

224
00:12:55,700 --> 00:12:58,206
Glow round bright lights, off and on.

225
00:12:58,206 --> 00:13:03,150
And N switches to the first version of the renderer, without any of this.

226
00:13:06,617 --> 00:13:10,298
A mirror needs something the rasteriser does not have:

227
00:13:10,298 --> 00:13:12,071
what is behind the camera.

228
00:13:12,071 --> 00:13:12,820
A ray does.

229
00:13:12,820 --> 00:13:14,661
Send a ray through a pixel.

230
00:13:14,661 --> 00:13:21,615
Where it hits, send one more ray to the light, one reflected ray, and through glass one refracted ray.

231
00:13:21,615 --> 00:13:31,705
Every part of the camp is a sphere, a box, a cylinder or a cone, so each hit is solved exactly, and a tree of boxes skips what the ray cannot reach.

232
00:13:32,617 --> 00:13:36,089
The semicolon key changes how the frame is made.

233
00:13:36,089 --> 00:13:37,609
First the rasteriser.

234
00:13:38,517 --> 00:13:39,012
Hybrid:

235
00:13:39,012 --> 00:13:41,840
rasterised, with ray-traced reflections.

236
00:13:41,840 --> 00:13:45,375
Whitted ray tracing, with refraction in the glass.

237
00:13:45,375 --> 00:13:48,980
Path tracing, where light bounces between surfaces.

238
00:13:48,980 --> 00:13:52,021
And light tracing, from the lamps outwards.

239
00:13:55,517 --> 00:14:01,021
With the ray debugger, one click shows the whole tree of rays behind a single pixel.

240
00:14:04,517 --> 00:14:05,172
Compared:

241
00:14:05,172 --> 00:14:09,326
screen-space reflections, a hybrid, and full ray tracing.

242
00:14:09,326 --> 00:14:09,836
Chosen:

243
00:14:09,836 --> 00:14:17,487
the rasteriser with screen-space reflections for playing, and the ray tracers as modes you can switch to.

244
00:14:18,383 --> 00:14:19,351
Question four.

245
00:14:19,351 --> 00:14:22,739
A lit picture of a place is still only a picture.

246
00:14:22,739 --> 00:14:24,261
What makes it a world?

247
00:14:24,261 --> 00:14:28,133
Time, weather, sound, and things that go on without you.

248
00:14:29,017 --> 00:14:29,747
Time first.

249
00:14:29,747 --> 00:14:33,665
The outpost stands at twenty-two point eight degrees north.

250
00:14:33,665 --> 00:14:37,184
From the hour, the program computes where the sun is.

251
00:14:37,184 --> 00:14:42,496
Low sun means a long path through the air, so the light turns orange and weaker.

252
00:14:42,496 --> 00:14:49,667
That one number, the height of the sun, drives the colour of the light, the sky, the lamps and the exposure.

253
00:14:50,583 --> 00:14:54,896
Here is one whole day in the program, in twenty-five seconds.

254
00:14:54,896 --> 00:14:56,168
Dawn at about six.

255
00:14:56,168 --> 00:14:57,865
The day shift comes out.

256
00:14:57,865 --> 00:14:58,218
Noon.

257
00:14:58,218 --> 00:15:00,480
The long light of the afternoon.

258
00:15:00,480 --> 00:15:05,005
At dusk the lamps come on, the drones lift off, and night falls.

259
00:15:15,583 --> 00:15:17,194
The R key brings rain:

260
00:15:17,194 --> 00:15:19,610
drops, darker and shinier ground.

261
00:15:19,610 --> 00:15:24,223
And at night, J switches the fireflies and the dust in the air.

262
00:15:29,583 --> 00:15:34,386
The ground is covered by four hundred and fifty thousand blades of grass.

263
00:15:34,386 --> 00:15:37,281
That would be far too many separate objects.

264
00:15:37,281 --> 00:15:44,716
One blade, seven corners and five triangles, is sent to the graphics card once, with a list of places to draw it.

265
00:15:44,716 --> 00:15:49,783
The trees at the two highest presets are the one thing that is not generated:

266
00:15:49,783 --> 00:15:51,756
they are public-domain meshes.

267
00:15:51,756 --> 00:15:56,165
Far away they are drawn simpler, and the farthest as flat pictures.

268
00:15:57,050 --> 00:15:58,324
Down in the grass.

269
00:15:58,324 --> 00:16:00,447
Every blade bends in the wind.

270
00:16:00,447 --> 00:16:07,311
F5 steps through the quality presets, from the lowest, which runs on a laptop, up to the highest.

271
00:16:11,050 --> 00:16:16,703
Measured on the test machine, the same scene costs this much time per frame at each preset.

272
00:16:18,050 --> 00:16:20,318
There are no sound files either.

273
00:16:20,318 --> 00:16:25,210
A cannon shot is a burst of noise through a filter, with a low thump.

274
00:16:25,210 --> 00:16:29,180
A rotor is a stack of tones at the rate the blades pass.

275
00:16:29,180 --> 00:16:35,772
Far sounds arrive late, because sound is slow, and dull, because air swallows the high tones.

276
00:16:35,772 --> 00:16:39,033
Loud shots echo off the wall and the treeline.

277
00:16:39,033 --> 00:16:43,074
Inside the bunker the same shot rings in a concrete room.

278
00:16:43,983 --> 00:16:44,951
Question five.

279
00:16:44,951 --> 00:16:45,918
Can you go in?

280
00:16:45,918 --> 00:16:48,681
A world you can only look at is a model.

281
00:16:48,681 --> 00:16:51,514
This one can be walked, driven and flown.

282
00:16:52,417 --> 00:16:56,077
Under the soldiers there is a second, invisible map:

283
00:16:56,077 --> 00:17:02,342
the floors they may stand on, the walls that stop them, and the doorways that join rooms.

284
00:17:02,342 --> 00:17:04,383
A door is a gate in that map.

285
00:17:04,383 --> 00:17:07,691
It opens when a soldier is close and facing it.

286
00:17:08,583 --> 00:17:12,769
Here a soldier under the player's control walks down the ramp.

287
00:17:12,769 --> 00:17:14,727
The blast gate opens for him.

288
00:17:15,617 --> 00:17:18,966
Through the tunnel, into the command room under the ground.

289
00:17:22,617 --> 00:17:26,065
Other doors are opened by hand, with the E key:

290
00:17:26,065 --> 00:17:27,459
the generator room.

291
00:17:28,617 --> 00:17:33,118
And the barracks hall, where the soldiers who are off duty rest and train.

292
00:17:39,117 --> 00:17:42,192
Twenty things in the camp can be operated.

293
00:17:42,192 --> 00:17:44,242
The rule is always the same.

294
00:17:44,242 --> 00:17:47,684
Select it, take control, and its own keys work:

295
00:17:47,684 --> 00:17:50,173
drive, aim, fire, a second action.

296
00:17:50,173 --> 00:17:54,347
V changes the camera, from outside to the gunner's sight.

297
00:17:54,347 --> 00:17:55,885
Escape gives it back.

298
00:17:56,783 --> 00:17:57,407
The tank.

299
00:17:57,407 --> 00:17:59,557
He walks up and gets in with E.

300
00:18:02,283 --> 00:18:06,742
Out of the bay, a turn on the spot, and the turret swings to the target.

301
00:18:12,283 --> 00:18:13,819
From the gunner's sight:

302
00:18:13,819 --> 00:18:14,523
two rounds.

303
00:18:20,483 --> 00:18:22,162
The anti-aircraft gun:

304
00:18:22,162 --> 00:18:24,451
six barrels, bursts of tracer.

305
00:18:25,483 --> 00:18:27,425
The surface-to-air missile launcher.

306
00:18:29,883 --> 00:18:30,912
The helicopter.

307
00:18:30,912 --> 00:18:33,382
Its second action starts the engine.

308
00:18:33,382 --> 00:18:38,459
The rotor spools up, and it lifts off, turns and flies out over the fence.

309
00:18:49,883 --> 00:18:52,307
A drone, taken off its pad by hand.

310
00:18:52,307 --> 00:18:54,661
V steps through its three cameras:

311
00:18:54,661 --> 00:18:58,609
behind it, from its nose, and through its thermal camera.

312
00:19:06,883 --> 00:19:09,625
Every moving thing has a point of view:

313
00:19:09,625 --> 00:19:15,249
each soldier, each vehicle, both drones, every intruder, even a round in flight.

314
00:19:15,249 --> 00:19:19,396
A click on the screen is turned into a ray from the camera.

315
00:19:19,396 --> 00:19:22,841
The first object the ray hits is the one you get.

316
00:19:23,750 --> 00:19:25,898
A soldier is walking his patrol.

317
00:19:25,898 --> 00:19:28,315
Move the pointer onto him and click.

318
00:19:30,750 --> 00:19:32,397
Now you see what he sees.

319
00:19:32,397 --> 00:19:36,681
He keeps walking his patrol, and you ride along, in first person.

320
00:19:38,250 --> 00:19:41,429
V steps back behind him, into third person.

321
00:19:44,250 --> 00:19:47,719
F4 lists everything that moves, nearest first.

322
00:19:47,719 --> 00:19:48,623
Click a row.

323
00:19:52,250 --> 00:19:55,629
The same jump works for anything that moves.

324
00:19:55,629 --> 00:19:56,167
A tank.

325
00:19:56,167 --> 00:19:57,319
The helicopter.

326
00:19:57,319 --> 00:19:58,778
A drone on its pad.

327
00:20:02,250 --> 00:20:04,061
At night it becomes a game.

328
00:20:04,061 --> 00:20:06,879
The list again, and into a drone's camera.

329
00:20:10,850 --> 00:20:14,455
A round is launched, and you ride it down to the target.

330
00:20:17,650 --> 00:20:19,159
Or take the other side:

331
00:20:19,159 --> 00:20:21,981
into an intruder, while the drones hunt it.

332
00:20:23,450 --> 00:20:26,245
Or a sentry at the gate, with his rifle.

333
00:20:29,850 --> 00:20:32,669
The soldiers are not controlled by anyone.

334
00:20:32,669 --> 00:20:36,428
Each has a duty for the day shift and one for the night.

335
00:20:36,428 --> 00:20:45,557
To walk there, he searches a grid for the shortest way round the obstacles, straightens the result, and rounds the corners into a curve.

336
00:20:45,557 --> 00:20:48,175
He slows down where the curve is tight.

337
00:20:49,083 --> 00:20:51,047
His legs are solved backwards.

338
00:20:51,047 --> 00:20:54,255
The planted foot stays where it is on the ground.

339
00:20:54,255 --> 00:20:59,819
From the hip and the foot, the knee angle follows from the lengths of thigh and shin.

340
00:20:59,819 --> 00:21:02,438
The other foot swings forward in an arc.

341
00:21:03,350 --> 00:21:03,815
By day:

342
00:21:03,815 --> 00:21:06,073
a mechanic works at a parked tank.

343
00:21:06,073 --> 00:21:08,597
Another carries crates from the depot.

344
00:21:08,597 --> 00:21:10,390
Sentries stand at the gate.

345
00:21:16,350 --> 00:21:20,114
When the camp is on alert, the weapons are manned and ready.

346
00:21:20,114 --> 00:21:22,686
Missile canisters raised through the net.

347
00:21:23,583 --> 00:21:26,127
The rocket pod at firing elevation.

348
00:21:26,127 --> 00:21:28,234
The gun searching its sector.

349
00:21:29,150 --> 00:21:32,201
Tanks in their bays with their guns over the wall.

350
00:21:33,117 --> 00:21:33,780
Compared:

351
00:21:33,780 --> 00:21:37,097
a scripted film of the camp, or a simulation.

352
00:21:37,097 --> 00:21:37,613
Chosen:

353
00:21:37,613 --> 00:21:38,571
a simulation.

354
00:21:38,571 --> 00:21:42,256
Nothing you have seen is an animation played back.

355
00:21:42,256 --> 00:21:48,669
Soldiers, doors, vehicles and time all run by rules, and you can step into any of them.

356
00:21:49,583 --> 00:21:50,487
Question six.

357
00:21:50,487 --> 00:21:54,660
The drones must find an intruder in the dark, by themselves.

358
00:21:54,660 --> 00:22:00,015
What does a machine see, and how does it decide that a warm spot is a target?

359
00:22:00,917 --> 00:22:03,481
A thermal camera does not see light.

360
00:22:03,481 --> 00:22:04,407
It sees heat.

361
00:22:04,407 --> 00:22:07,327
Every part in the camp has a temperature:

362
00:22:07,327 --> 00:22:11,315
an engine is hot, a soldier is warm, the ground is cool.

363
00:22:11,315 --> 00:22:15,660
A real sensor is not perfect, so the program models that too:

364
00:22:15,660 --> 00:22:23,423
the lens blurs the picture, every pixel has its own small error, columns show as stripes, and there is noise.

365
00:22:24,317 --> 00:22:26,923
F switches the whole view to thermal.

366
00:22:26,923 --> 00:22:27,909
Hot is bright.

367
00:22:27,909 --> 00:22:29,458
T changes the palette.

368
00:22:34,317 --> 00:22:37,650
And this is what a drone's own camera sends down:

369
00:22:37,650 --> 00:22:42,615
three hundred and twenty by two hundred and forty pixels, with its noise.

370
00:22:43,517 --> 00:22:47,935
Every frame of that picture goes through the same five steps.

371
00:22:47,935 --> 00:22:48,225
One:

372
00:22:48,225 --> 00:22:49,094
a threshold.

373
00:22:49,094 --> 00:22:58,872
Keep only pixels above eighty per cent heat, or above the level that best splits the picture into dark and bright, whichever is higher.

374
00:22:58,872 --> 00:22:59,162
Two:

375
00:22:59,162 --> 00:23:01,118
remove single stray pixels.

376
00:23:01,118 --> 00:23:01,552
Three:

377
00:23:01,552 --> 00:23:04,595
join neighbouring hot pixels into regions.

378
00:23:04,595 --> 00:23:04,957
Four:

379
00:23:04,957 --> 00:23:06,405
measure each region.

380
00:23:06,405 --> 00:23:06,768
Five:

381
00:23:06,768 --> 00:23:08,071
decide what it is.

382
00:23:08,983 --> 00:23:15,240
Measuring a region gives its area, its centre, its hottest pixel and how stretched it is.

383
00:23:15,240 --> 00:23:16,224
Then the rule.

384
00:23:16,224 --> 00:23:19,529
Small, with a very hot core, not too stretched:

385
00:23:19,529 --> 00:23:20,935
a vehicle, a target.

386
00:23:20,935 --> 00:23:24,098
Warm, without a hot core, upright or compact:

387
00:23:24,098 --> 00:23:26,980
a person, and a person is never fired at.

388
00:23:26,980 --> 00:23:28,738
Anything else is clutter.

389
00:23:29,650 --> 00:23:31,243
By day you can check it.

390
00:23:31,243 --> 00:23:35,360
Through the drone's camera, the soldiers are boxed as persons.

391
00:23:35,360 --> 00:23:37,351
None of them is ever a target.

392
00:23:38,250 --> 00:23:40,137
One detection is not enough:

393
00:23:40,137 --> 00:23:44,719
the camera shakes, pixels flicker, and a rover drives behind a tree.

394
00:23:44,719 --> 00:23:49,773
So each target becomes a track with a position, a speed and an uncertainty.

395
00:23:49,773 --> 00:23:55,164
Between frames the track is moved ahead by its speed, and the uncertainty grows.

396
00:23:55,164 --> 00:23:59,139
A new detection pulls it back, and the uncertainty shrinks.

397
00:23:59,139 --> 00:24:00,757
This is a Kalman filter.

398
00:24:01,650 --> 00:24:04,248
Which detection belongs to which track?

399
00:24:04,248 --> 00:24:11,776
Only detections inside a track's gate may match, and the pairs are chosen so that the total distance is smallest.

400
00:24:11,776 --> 00:24:20,637
When both drones see the same target, their two lines of sight are crossed, which fixes the position much better than one camera can.

401
00:24:21,550 --> 00:24:23,981
In the drone's view you can watch it.

402
00:24:23,981 --> 00:24:25,360
The boxes are tracks.

403
00:24:25,360 --> 00:24:33,113
When the rover goes behind cover, the track coasts on its prediction, and is picked up again when the rover comes out.

404
00:24:34,017 --> 00:24:36,338
Here is that same night as numbers:

405
00:24:36,338 --> 00:24:38,924
the uncertainty of one track over time.

406
00:24:38,924 --> 00:24:44,427
It jumps while the rover is hidden and falls back each time a camera sees it again.

407
00:24:45,317 --> 00:24:46,335
Question seven.

408
00:24:46,335 --> 00:24:50,067
Three kinds of things in the camp think for themselves:

409
00:24:50,067 --> 00:24:52,986
the drones, the intruders and the soldiers.

410
00:24:52,986 --> 00:24:56,175
What rules make them act as if they had a plan?

411
00:24:57,083 --> 00:25:00,371
A drone has a duty for each part of the day.

412
00:25:00,371 --> 00:25:02,239
Docked on its pad by day.

413
00:25:02,239 --> 00:25:05,602
At dusk the rotors spool up and it takes off.

414
00:25:05,602 --> 00:25:09,636
At night the two patrol on opposite sides of a circle.

415
00:25:09,636 --> 00:25:11,803
At dawn they return and land.

416
00:25:12,717 --> 00:25:13,031
Dusk.

417
00:25:13,031 --> 00:25:17,303
The lamps come on, and both drones lift off with their searchlights.

418
00:25:20,717 --> 00:25:23,511
On patrol, searchlights sweeping the camp.

419
00:25:25,717 --> 00:25:32,676
When a track is confirmed, the nearer drone becomes the lead and the other its wing, ninety degrees apart.

420
00:25:32,676 --> 00:25:40,358
The lead must hold its lock for eight tenths of a second, the wing for half a second, each with a clear line of fire.

421
00:25:40,358 --> 00:25:46,005
With one intruder, the lead waits up to eight seconds for the wing, so that both fire.

422
00:25:46,917 --> 00:25:49,404
They do not fire at the same moment.

423
00:25:49,404 --> 00:25:54,449
The round from the farther drone needs longer, so that drone fires first.

424
00:25:54,449 --> 00:25:56,384
Both rounds arrive together.

425
00:25:56,384 --> 00:25:58,181
That is a combined strike.

426
00:25:59,083 --> 00:25:59,860
Here it is.

427
00:25:59,860 --> 00:26:01,130
One intruder left.

428
00:26:01,130 --> 00:26:05,505
Both drones hold their locks, and both rounds arrive together.

429
00:26:07,583 --> 00:26:09,561
Now a second intruder appears.

430
00:26:09,561 --> 00:26:17,799
If it is more than fourteen metres from the first, and it is close to something it could damage, waiting would cost too much.

431
00:26:17,799 --> 00:26:18,854
The pair splits.

432
00:26:18,854 --> 00:26:22,742
The nearer drone takes the second target and strikes alone.

433
00:26:22,742 --> 00:26:28,213
When that target is gone, or the two come within nine metres, the pair forms again.

434
00:26:29,117 --> 00:26:30,844
The same night, earlier.

435
00:26:30,844 --> 00:26:32,571
Two intruders far apart.

436
00:26:32,571 --> 00:26:35,666
The pair splits, and one drone fires alone.

437
00:26:37,117 --> 00:26:38,710
And the guards join in:

438
00:26:38,710 --> 00:26:44,391
a soldier at his post opens fire on any tracked intruder within twenty-six metres.

439
00:26:45,283 --> 00:26:45,979
Compared:

440
00:26:45,979 --> 00:26:49,381
always one drone per target, or always both.

441
00:26:49,381 --> 00:26:49,922
Chosen:

442
00:26:49,922 --> 00:26:51,623
a rule that weighs it.

443
00:26:51,623 --> 00:26:58,041
Both rounds for a lone intruder, one each when a second one is far away and urgent.

444
00:26:58,950 --> 00:27:00,159
Now change sides.

445
00:27:00,159 --> 00:27:06,562
An intruder is a small rover that wants to reach something in the camp without being seen.

446
00:27:06,562 --> 00:27:08,768
It lays a grid over the ground:

447
00:27:08,768 --> 00:27:11,756
eighty by eighty cells, half a metre each.

448
00:27:11,756 --> 00:27:14,246
Cells inside obstacles are blocked.

449
00:27:15,150 --> 00:27:17,121
Every free cell gets a cost.

450
00:27:17,121 --> 00:27:18,318
One for the step.

451
00:27:18,318 --> 00:27:20,712
More if a drone can see that cell:

452
00:27:20,712 --> 00:27:27,892
the rover draws a line from each drone to the cell, and if nothing is in the way, the cell is exposed.

453
00:27:27,892 --> 00:27:33,454
And a little random cost of its own, so that no two rovers choose the same way.

454
00:27:34,350 --> 00:27:37,300
Then the trick from the MicroMouse maze robots.

455
00:27:37,300 --> 00:27:44,957
Start at the goal with zero and let the numbers flood outwards, each cell taking the cheapest neighbour plus its own cost.

456
00:27:44,957 --> 00:27:48,346
When the flood reaches the rover, the route is simply:

457
00:27:48,346 --> 00:27:51,673
always step to the neighbour with the smaller number.

458
00:27:52,583 --> 00:27:54,937
M shows this inside the program:

459
00:27:54,937 --> 00:27:59,276
the flood field as coloured cells, and the route as a line.

460
00:27:59,276 --> 00:28:00,967
Each rover has its own.

461
00:28:01,883 --> 00:28:03,677
A route is not a movement.

462
00:28:03,677 --> 00:28:07,539
The rover follows it the way a line-follower robot does.

463
00:28:07,539 --> 00:28:12,299
Five sensors across its nose each say whether they are over the line.

464
00:28:12,299 --> 00:28:14,782
Their weighted average is the error:

465
00:28:14,782 --> 00:28:16,989
how far the line is to one side.

466
00:28:16,989 --> 00:28:25,542
A controller turns that error, and how fast it changes, into a turn rate, and the two wheels are driven at different speeds.

467
00:28:26,450 --> 00:28:27,325
Close to one.

468
00:28:27,325 --> 00:28:31,026
It weaves a little, changes speed, and sometimes stops:

469
00:28:31,026 --> 00:28:34,727
so that a tracker cannot simply extend a straight line.

470
00:28:36,450 --> 00:28:39,309
And this is the view from its own mast camera.

471
00:28:42,450 --> 00:28:43,469
And it listens.

472
00:28:43,469 --> 00:28:48,360
When a drone comes within five metres, the rover stops what it is doing.

473
00:28:48,360 --> 00:28:50,466
Its goal is no longer the camp.

474
00:28:50,466 --> 00:28:54,134
Its goal becomes every cell that the drone cannot see:

475
00:28:54,134 --> 00:28:56,919
under a tree, behind a wall, under a net.

476
00:28:56,919 --> 00:29:02,965
The same flood fill runs again from those cells, and the rover drives to the nearest one.

477
00:29:02,965 --> 00:29:08,263
It waits four seconds, and plans again, because by then the drones have moved.

478
00:29:09,150 --> 00:29:11,292
Three views of the same seconds.

479
00:29:11,292 --> 00:29:13,970
On the left, the drone's thermal camera.

480
00:29:13,970 --> 00:29:16,313
In the middle, the rover's own map:

481
00:29:16,313 --> 00:29:21,133
red where a drone can see, dark where it is hidden, the route in yellow.

482
00:29:21,133 --> 00:29:23,008
On the right, the wide view.

483
00:29:23,008 --> 00:29:27,091
Watch the route bend into the shadow as the drone comes near.

484
00:29:27,983 --> 00:29:28,653
Compared:

485
00:29:28,653 --> 00:29:33,489
a fixed path, a shortest path, and a path that prices being seen.

486
00:29:33,489 --> 00:29:34,010
Chosen:

487
00:29:34,010 --> 00:29:39,887
flood fill on a cost map that changes as the drones move, followed like a line.

488
00:29:40,783 --> 00:29:43,951
Put together, it is a game called Night Watch.

489
00:29:43,951 --> 00:29:47,533
Rovers come in waves, two more each time, up to six.

490
00:29:47,533 --> 00:29:52,216
Each has one hundred points of armour, and a hit takes about thirty.

491
00:29:52,216 --> 00:29:56,693
A kill scores one hundred, a combined kill one hundred and fifty.

492
00:29:56,693 --> 00:30:00,687
A rover that reaches its goal costs the base ten per cent.

493
00:30:01,583 --> 00:30:03,791
The blast itself is physics too.

494
00:30:03,791 --> 00:30:07,656
Hundreds of particles are thrown out with random speeds.

495
00:30:07,656 --> 00:30:12,693
Gravity pulls them down, drag slows them, they bounce once on the ground.

496
00:30:12,693 --> 00:30:16,488
Each one cools, and its colour follows its temperature:

497
00:30:16,488 --> 00:30:19,525
white, yellow, orange, red, then dark smoke.

498
00:30:20,417 --> 00:30:22,443
The wreck burns, and the fire cools.

499
00:30:26,417 --> 00:30:30,726
At dawn the rovers that are left withdraw, and the drones come home.

500
00:30:34,417 --> 00:30:35,384
Question eight.

501
00:30:35,384 --> 00:30:35,900
It runs.

502
00:30:35,900 --> 00:30:39,771
How do you know it is right, and what went wrong on the way?

503
00:30:40,683 --> 00:30:42,750
Four problems took the longest.

504
00:30:42,750 --> 00:30:48,015
Far surfaces flickered, because depth is stored most precisely near the camera.

505
00:30:48,015 --> 00:30:51,681
Shadows had stripes, because a surface shadowed itself.

506
00:30:51,681 --> 00:30:54,414
Thin wires shimmered from frame to frame.

507
00:30:54,414 --> 00:30:59,413
And soldiers walked through walls, until doors became part of the walk map.

508
00:30:59,413 --> 00:31:04,811
Each one is in the report with its cause, the fix and a picture before and after.

509
00:31:05,717 --> 00:31:08,511
Every claim in this film can be checked.

510
00:31:08,511 --> 00:31:12,982
Three hundred and ninety-six automatic tests run on every build.

511
00:31:12,982 --> 00:31:18,501
The same seed always gives exactly the same night, so a result can be repeated.

512
00:31:18,501 --> 00:31:21,994
A whole play session can be recorded and replayed.

513
00:31:22,883 --> 00:31:24,957
And every number has one home:

514
00:31:24,957 --> 00:31:25,718
a workbook.

515
00:31:25,718 --> 00:31:31,871
Each coordinate of each part, each constant of each algorithm, each number in the report.

516
00:31:31,871 --> 00:31:41,273
The report takes its numbers from the workbook, and a checker fails the build if the program, the workbook and the report ever disagree.

517
00:31:42,183 --> 00:31:42,631
To end:

518
00:31:42,631 --> 00:31:45,447
one night, without a cut and without a word.

519
00:32:13,783 --> 00:32:15,626
From one triangle to this:

520
00:32:15,626 --> 00:32:28,098
sixteen generators, five thousand four hundred and seventy-four parts, a full pipeline with its mathematics written out, a thermal camera, a tracker, and three kinds of agents.

521
00:32:28,983 --> 00:32:29,896
ThermalSense.

522
00:32:29,896 --> 00:32:35,719
Computer Graphics Laboratory, Department of Computer Science and Engineering, KUET.

523
00:32:35,719 --> 00:32:37,965
Everything is in the repository:

524
00:32:37,965 --> 00:32:42,385
the code, the report, the workbook, and this film's own script.

525
00:32:42,385 --> 00:32:43,789
Now it is your turn.
